  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nOur approach leverages the recently developed compactly supported smooth pulse solutions to achieve a tighter bound. The key idea is to represent the step function h(x) using a finite number of overlapping Gaussian pulses, tuned to minimize the maximum overlap. This method provides a smooth, yet compact representation that effectively captures the structure of the optimal function while maintaining feasibility. The rationale is that the current approach may be trapped in local optimality due to its reliance on gradient-based methods and linear interpolation during resolution transfer. The pulse-based representation introduces a genuinely new candidate that can escape local optima and potentially achieve a better overlap integral lower bound of 0.3808.\n\nMathematical specification:\nVariables:\n- h(x): Step function defined on [0,2] taking values in [0,1]\n- t_k: Position of the k-th Gaussian pulse\n- \u03c3: Standard deviation of the Gaussian pulses\n- A_k: Amplitude coefficient for each pulse\n\nObjective function in terms of pulse parameters:\nC5 = max_lag \u222b_0^2 h(x) (1 - h(x+\u0394x)) dx\n\nConstraints:\n1. \u222b_0\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n2. 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n\nWe parameterize h(x) as a sum of N Gaussian pulses:\n\nh(x) = 1/2 + \u03a3_{k=1}^N A_k exp(-(x - t_k)^2 / (2\u03c3^2)) \n\nwhere A_k is chosen to maintain 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1. This is implemented through normalization: with increasing N, the amplitude decreases proportionally.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Parameterization:\n   - Select a fixed position grid t_k = k*(2/(N-1)) - 1 for k=-N/2+1 to N/2 (symmetric placement)\n   - Define initial pulse position distribution as uniform grid\n   - Use initial \u03c3 and A_k-values based on the known parent solution (360 points)\n\n2. Optimization loop:\n   - Use constrained optimization along each parameter dimension sequentially\n   - For each pulse k:\n     * Optimize amplitude A_k under constraints\n     * Optimize position t_k globally with step size control\n     * Optimize \u03c3 using bi-section method\n\n3. Candidate projection:\n   - Ensure 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 by clipping and re-normalizing\n   - Maintain integral constraint through \u03c3 adjustment\n   - Compute overlap integral to score each iteration\n\n4. Multiple initializations:\n   - Initialize with different pulse amplitude profiles\n   - Use random position jitter to escape local optima\n   - Warm-start with existing parent solution transfer\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n1. Sweeps:\n   - \u03c3 values: [0.1, 0.2, 0.3]\n   - \u03b7 parameter in position adjustment range(0.01, 0.03)\n   - A_k loss: 0.95 to 1.0 (with 0.95 being more homogeneous)\n\n2. Learning schedule:\n   - Initial sampling density: high at boundaries (modify to lambda-based distribution)\n   - Decreasing learning rate with time\n   - Polynomial kernels for smoothness control\n\n3. Stoppers:\n   - Convergence in top 5% of solutions\n   - Stability test over 3 optimization rounds\n   - \u03c3 near analytical optimal\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\nEstimate time allocation:\n- Parameter space exploration: 50%\n- Local improvement: 30%\n- Resolution verification: 20%\n\nCheck timeout during:\n- Optimization rounds: every 10 minutes\n- Parameter sweeps: every 5 minutes\n- Validation: when entering final refinement\n\nFallback procedure on timeout:\n1. Return best-known parent solution\n2. Keep track of failed parameters\n3. Resume with most productive variation\n\nValidation and return contract:\n1. Compute C5 using original correlation method\n2. Verify: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 with tolerance 1e-8\n3. Ensure \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1 with tolerance 1e-8\n4. Re-evaluate overlapping integral using exact same method as parent\n5. Store intermediate best solutions to restart on recovery\n\nImplementation details:\n\u0430) Pulse construction:\nFor given parameters {t_k, \u03c3, A_k}, construct h(x) as:\n   h(x) = 1/2 + sum_{k=1}^{N} A_k * exp(- (x - t_k)^2 / (2*\u03c3^2))\nNormalize: scale such that integral equals 1\n\nb) Constraint handling:\nBorders: clip to [0,1] using softmax transformation\nGradient implementation: finite-difference-based in core code\n\nc) Parameter transfers:\nGrid size: N=240, 360, 480 (as parent is 360)\nProjection: use bilinear interpolation rather than previous method\nConstraint projection: orthogonal projection preserving integral\n\nd) Candidate selection:\nCache top 10% of solutions per optimization round\nPersist solutions between algorithm restarts\nMulti-metric evaluation (not just C5): improvement rate, stability\n\ne) Reporting:\nStore list of evaluated solutions for top tuning\nTrack improvement trajectory in validation ramp\nMonitor convergence via log-likelihood (bound tightening)\n\nThe algorithm lends itself to parallel implementation and has proven effective in achieving lower bounds for similar optimization problems. The approach has the potential to break current performance barriers by explicitly constructing the optimal structure rather than through many local optimization steps.",
